<!-- What is TIE Card -->
<section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
        <div class="msv__section-hd">
            <h2>What is the TIE Card?</h2>
            <p>The TIE (Tarjeta de Identidad de Extranjero) is Spain's official foreigner identity card. It's a biometric ID document that serves as your primary proof of legal residence in Spain for all non-EU citizens.</p>
        </div>

        <p>When you arrive in Spain on any long-term visa or residence permit—whether it's a Non-Lucrative Visa, Digital Nomad Visa, Student Visa, or Work Visa—you don't legally exist in Spain's system without a TIE card. Your visa sticker in your passport alone is not sufficient. The TIE replaces that visa sticker as your official identification and proof that you're authorized to live and work in Spain.</p>

        <p>The TIE card is a physical, laminated biometric card similar in size and format to a driver's license. It contains your photograph, fingerprints, personal details, and your NIE (Número de Identidad de Extranjero) number. For <a href="/nlv-pillar-page.html">Non-Lucrative Visa holders</a>, <a href="/dnv-pillar-page.html">Digital Nomads</a>, and other residence permit holders, this card becomes your essential legal document.</p>

        <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--info">
            <span class="msv__alert-icon">ℹ️</span>
            <p><strong>The TIE replaces the visa sticker in your passport</strong> as your proof of legal residence in Spain. Once you have a TIE, you no longer rely on the physical visa stamp for identification within Spain.</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Why the TIE Matters</h3>
        <p>You'll need your TIE card for:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Opening a bank account in Spain</li>
            <li>Signing employment contracts and registering with tax authorities</li>
            <li>Healthcare registration and social security enrollment</li>
            <li>Renting or purchasing property</li>
            <li>Any official government or administrative interaction</li>
            <li>Proving legal residence status to employers and institutions</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Without a valid TIE, many institutions in Spain will not work with you, even if your visa is current. Banks will refuse to open accounts. Employers may not hire you. The TIE is non-negotiable.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- TIE vs NIE -->
<section class="msv__section msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
        <div class="msv__section-hd">
            <h2>TIE vs NIE—What's the Difference?</h2>
            <p>These two terms are often confused, but they're different things. Here's what you need to know.</p>
        </div>

        <table class="msv__comparison-table">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Feature</th>
                    <th>TIE (Tarjeta)</th>
                    <th>NIE (Number)</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td class="table-feature">What it is</td>
                    <td>Physical biometric identity card</td>
                    <td>Tax identification number</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="table-feature">Format</td>
                    <td>Laminated card with photo & fingerprints</td>
                    <td>9-digit alphanumeric code (e.g., X1234567A)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="table-feature">Mandatory for</td>
                    <td>All non-EU residents with long-term visas</td>
                    <td>Anyone working, self-employed, or doing business in Spain</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="table-feature">Purpose</td>
                    <td>Proof of legal residence in Spain</td>
                    <td>Tax administration and financial transactions</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="table-feature">Application</td>
                    <td>At National Police (Policía Nacional)</td>
                    <td>At the National Police or through delegations</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="table-feature">Cost</td>
                    <td>€16 (Tasa 012)</td>
                    <td>Free (usually obtained simultaneously with TIE)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="table-feature">Renewal</td>
                    <td>When residence permit expires</td>
                    <td>Doesn't expire—stays with you permanently</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p style="margin-top: 2rem;"><strong>In practice:</strong> You'll need both. Your NIE (the number) is automatically assigned when you apply for your TIE card. The TIE card is the physical document that proves your legal status; the NIE is your tax and administration identifier. <a href="/nie-number-spain.html">Learn more about the NIE number in detail here.</a></p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- Who Needs a TIE -->
<section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
        <div class="msv__section-hd">
            <h2>Who Needs a TIE Card?</h2>
            <p>If you're a non-EU citizen with a long-term visa or residence permit, you must obtain a TIE card.</p>
        </div>

        <p><strong>You need a TIE if you hold any of these:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li><a href="/nlv-pillar-page.html">Non-Lucrative Visa (NLV)</a>—for those with passive income or sufficient funds</li>
            <li><a href="/dnv-pillar-page.html">Digital Nomad Visa (DNV)</a>—for remote workers and freelancers</li>
            <li><a href="/student-visa-pillar-page.html">Student Visa</a>—for university enrollment or language courses</li>
            <li>Work Visa or employment contract authorization</li>
            <li>Entrepreneur/Self-Employment Visa</li>
            <li>Family Reunification Residence Permit</li>
            <li>Special circumstances residence permits (e.g., victims of trafficking, investors)</li>
            <li><a href="/spain-residence-permit.html">Any form of long-term residence permit</a></li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>You do NOT need a TIE if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>You're an EU/EEA citizen (you have different rights and don't need a separate TIE)</li>
            <li>You're on a tourist visa or short-stay permit (under 90 days)</li>
            <li>You're not yet officially registered as a resident</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The moment you activate your long-term residence permit in Spain, you're required to apply for a TIE. This is a legal obligation, not optional.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- How to Apply Process -->
<section class="msv__section msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
        <div class="msv__section-hd">
            <h2>How to Apply for Your TIE Card</h2>
            <p>The process is straightforward but has specific steps and deadlines. Here's exactly what you need to do.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__process-steps">
            <div class="msv__process-step">
                <div class="msv__process-step-num">1</div>
                <div class="msv__process-step-title">Gather Documents</div>
            </div>
            <div class="msv__process-step">
                <div class="msv__process-step-num">2</div>
                <div class="msv__process-step-title">Complete EX-17 Form</div>
            </div>
            <div class="msv__process-step">
                <div class="msv__process-step-num">3</div>
                <div class="msv__process-step-title">Pay Tasa 012 Fee</div>
            </div>
            <div class="msv__process-step">
                <div class="msv__process-step-num">4</div>
                <div class="msv__process-step-title">Book Cita Previa</div>
            </div>
            <div class="msv__process-step">
                <div class="msv__process-step-num">5</div>
                <div class="msv__process-step-title">Attend Appointment</div>
            </div>
            <div class="msv__process-step">
                <div class="msv__process-step-num">6</div>
                <div class="msv__process-step-title">Collect Your TIE</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Step-by-Step Breakdown</h3>

        <h4 style="font-weight: 700; font-size: 1rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Step 1: Gather Your Documents</h4>
        <p>Before you do anything, collect all required documents. You'll need originals and certified copies (with apostille for documents issued outside Spain). See the full documents section below for the complete list, but you'll need:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Your original passport and a photocopy (all pages)</li>
            <li>Your residence permit authorization (original and copy)</li>
            <li>Completed and signed EX-17 form (4 copies)</li>
            <li>Proof of payment for Tasa 012 (€16)</li>
            <li>Proof of address in Spain (rental contract, electricity bill, or rental agreement)</li>
            <li>2 colored passport photos (4x4 cm)</li>
            <li>If applicable: NIE assignment document (if you already have one)</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 style="font-weight: 700; font-size: 1rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Step 2: Complete the EX-17 Form</h4>
        <p>The EX-17 form is your formal TIE application. You can download it from the Spanish National Police website (policia.es) or from any police headquarters. Fill it out completely and accurately—errors can delay your application. You'll need 4 copies of this form.</p>

        <h4 style="font-weight: 700; font-size: 1rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Step 3: Pay the Tasa 012 Fee</h4>
        <p>The TIE application fee (Tasa 012) is currently €16. You can pay this at most Spanish banks or online. Keep your payment receipt—you'll need to submit it with your application. Payment must be completed before your appointment.</p>

        <h4 style="font-weight: 700; font-size: 1rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Step 4: Book a Cita Previa (Appointment)</h4>
        <p>Once documents are ready and fees are paid, you need to book an appointment at your local National Police headquarters (Comisaría de Policía Nacional). You can book appointments at:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Official website:</strong> cita-previa.policia.es (search for "expedición de tarjeta de identidad de extranjero")</li>
            <li><strong>In person:</strong> Visit your local police headquarters</li>
            <li><strong>By phone:</strong> Call the non-emergency police line in your area</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Appointments can book up quickly, especially in major cities. Book as soon as documents are ready. You'll select your appointment date and time and receive a reference number.</p>

        <h4 style="font-weight: 700; font-size: 1rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Step 5: Attend Your Appointment</h4>
        <p>On your appointment day, bring all required documents to the police headquarters at the scheduled time. The appointment is brief—they'll verify your documents, take your photograph and fingerprints, and collect all paperwork. You'll be given a receipt confirming your TIE application.</p>
        <p>Be on time. Appointments are strict. If you miss yours, you'll need to book another, and wait times can be weeks.</p>

        <h4 style="font-weight: 700; font-size: 1rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Step 6: Collect Your TIE Card</h4>
        <p>After 4–6 weeks, your TIE will be ready for collection. You'll receive a notification (or can call to check status). Return to the same police headquarters to collect your card. You'll need your receipt from the appointment. Once you have your TIE in hand, you're legally settled.</p>

        <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
            <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
            <p><strong>Critical deadline:</strong> You must apply for your TIE within 30 days of entering Spain. Missing this deadline can result in fines and complications with your legal status. Apply immediately after you arrive and your residence permit is activated.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- Documents Required -->
<section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
        <div class="msv__section-hd">
            <h2>Documents You'll Need</h2>
            <p>Have these ready before booking your appointment. All non-Spanish documents must be officially certified with an apostille.</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Original Documents to Bring</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Passport:</strong> Valid passport (original + 2 photocopies of all pages)</li>
            <li><strong>Residence Permit Authorization:</strong> Original and photocopy of your visa or residence permit approval</li>
            <li><strong>NIE Assignment (if already obtained):</strong> Copy of your NIE document</li>
            <li><strong>Proof of Address:</strong> Lease agreement, utility bill, registration with local council (padrón), or property deed—must show your name and current address</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Forms and Payments</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>EX-17 Application Form:</strong> 4 complete, signed, and dated copies (download from policia.es)</li>
            <li><strong>Tasa 012 Payment Receipt:</strong> Proof of payment for the €16 application fee</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Photographs</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Passport Photos:</strong> 2 recent colored photographs (4 x 4 cm), taken within last 6 months, white background</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Certified Translations (if applicable)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>If your documents are in a language other than Spanish, you'll need officially certified Spanish translations prepared by a sworn translator (traductor jurado)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Additional Documents (Depending on Your Situation)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>For minors:</strong> Birth certificate (certified copy with apostille) + consent from both parents</li>
            <li><strong>If you changed your name:</strong> Court decree or marriage certificate showing name change</li>
            <li><strong>If applying in a different region:</strong> Documentation permitting you to reside outside your initial entry point</li>
        </ul>

        <p style="margin-top: 2rem; padding: 1.3rem; background: #f9fafb; border-left: 3px solid var(--red); border-radius: 4px;">Always confirm the exact document requirements with your local National Police headquarters before your appointment, as requirements can vary slightly by region.</p>
    </div>
</section>

