<!-- Processing Time -->
<section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
        <div class="msv__section-hd">
            <h2>Processing Times</h2>
            <p>Here's what to expect from application to collection.</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Timeline</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Document preparation:</strong> 1–2 weeks (gathering and obtaining certifications)</li>
            <li><strong>Booking appointment:</strong> Immediate to 2 weeks (depends on availability in your area)</li>
            <li><strong>From appointment to collection:</strong> 4–6 weeks (average)</li>
            <li><strong>Total from start to finish:</strong> 6–10 weeks</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Important:</strong> Processing times can vary significantly by region. Madrid and Barcelona, which see high volumes, may take closer to 6–8 weeks. Smaller cities may process in 4–5 weeks. Some regions have experienced delays of up to 8–10 weeks during peak periods.</p>

        <p>You'll receive notification when your TIE is ready for collection. This is usually via email or SMS if you provided those contact details during your appointment. You can also call the police headquarters to check the status.</p>

        <p><strong>Can you speed this up?</strong> Not officially. The process follows government timelines. However, some gestorías (administrative management firms) offer services to handle the application on your behalf, which can occasionally expedite booking appointments in busy areas. <a href="/how-to-get-residency-in-spain.html">Learn more about working with residency specialists here.</a></p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- TIE Card Renewal -->
<section class="msv__section msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
        <div class="msv__section-hd">
            <h2>Renewing Your TIE Card</h2>
            <p>Your TIE is valid for as long as your residence permit is valid. When you renew your residence permit, you renew your TIE.</p>
        </div>

        <h3>When Do You Need to Renew?</h3>
        <p>Your TIE expires on the same date as your residence permit. If you hold a <a href="/nlv-pillar-page.html">Non-Lucrative Visa</a>, <a href="/dnv-pillar-page.html">Digital Nomad Visa</a>, or other residence permit, your TIE is valid for that same period. For example:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>NLV holders get a 1-year TIE (renewable annually)</li>
            <li>Digital Nomad Visa holders get a 2-year TIE (renewable for another 2 years)</li>
            <li>Student Visa holders get a TIE matching their study period</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Renewal Process</h3>
        <p>TIE renewal follows the same process as initial application:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>You renew your residence permit through the Spanish Immigration Office (Oficina de Extranjería)</li>
            <li>Once approved, you apply for TIE renewal at the National Police headquarters</li>
            <li>The process and documents required are nearly identical to initial application</li>
            <li>The cost remains €16</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Timing Is Critical</h3>
        <p>Apply for <a href="/spanish-residency-renewal.html">residence permit renewal at least 2–3 months before expiration</a>. If you apply after your TIE expires, you may face complications with your legal status and potential fines. It's much easier to renew before expiration than to deal with expired documentation.</p>

        <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--info">
            <span class="msv__alert-icon">ℹ️</span>
            <p><strong>Plan ahead:</strong> Start your residence permit renewal process 3 months before your TIE expires. This ensures you have a valid TIE throughout your residency in Spain.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- Travel with TIE -->
<section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
        <div class="msv__section-hd">
            <h2>Traveling with Your TIE Card</h2>
            <p>Important: The TIE is not a travel document. You cannot use it for international travel.</p>
        </div>

        <p><strong>Within Spain:</strong> Your TIE alone is sufficient identification within Spain. If police stop you or you need to prove legal residency, your TIE is your primary document. You should always carry it with you.</p>

        <p><strong>Leaving Spain or Europe:</strong> You must carry your passport for any international travel, regardless of whether you have a TIE. The TIE is proof of residence in Spain—it does nothing for you at airports, border crossings, or outside Spanish territory.</p>

        <p><strong>Traveling within the Schengen area:</strong> If you hold a long-term Spanish residence permit (like an NLV or DNV), you typically have Schengen travel rights. However, you still need your passport at borders. Your TIE cannot be used in place of your passport for travel.</p>

        <p><strong>Summary:</strong> Always carry your passport when traveling. Your TIE is for within Spain only.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- FAQ Section -->
<section class="msv__section msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
        <div class="msv__section-hd">
            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <p>Common questions about TIE cards answered.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__faq-list">
            <details>
                <summary>What exactly is a TIE card?</summary>
                <div class="msv__faq-answer">
                    <p>The TIE (Tarjeta de Identidad de Extranjero) is Spain's mandatory biometric identity card for non-EU residents. It's a physical laminated card with your photo, fingerprints, and personal details. It serves as your proof of legal residence in Spain and replaces the visa sticker in your passport for identification purposes within Spain.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <details>
                <summary>Do I legally need a TIE card if I have a valid visa?</summary>
                <div class="msv__faq-answer">
                    <p>Yes. Having a valid visa sticker in your passport is not sufficient. You must have a TIE card to be legally registered in Spain. Without it, you cannot open a bank account, work, rent property, or access public services. It's mandatory, not optional.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <details>
                <summary>What's the difference between a TIE and an NIE?</summary>
                <div class="msv__faq-answer">
                    <p>The TIE is a physical identity card. The NIE is a 9-digit tax identification number. You need both. The NIE is automatically assigned when you apply for your TIE. The NIE never expires; the TIE expires when your residence permit expires. See the detailed comparison table in the guide above.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <details>
                <summary>How much does a TIE card cost?</summary>
                <div class="msv__faq-answer">
                    <p>The application fee (Tasa 012) is €16. This covers the card production and issuance. Additional costs may apply if you need certified translations, apostilles, or notarized copies of documents. Total estimated cost is €16–50 in most cases.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <details>
                <summary>How long does it take to get a TIE card?</summary>
                <div class="msv__faq-answer">
                    <p>Processing typically takes 4–6 weeks from your appointment to collection. Add time for document preparation (1–2 weeks) and booking an appointment (immediate to 2 weeks), bringing the total timeline to 6–10 weeks from start to finish. Times vary by region.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <details>
                <summary>What happens if I miss the 30-day deadline to apply?</summary>
                <div class="msv__faq-answer">
                    <p>Missing the 30-day deadline is a serious violation of Spanish immigration law. It can result in fines, complications with your legal status, and difficulty renewing your residence permit. If you miss it, you should apply immediately and consult with an immigration attorney. Avoid this by applying as soon as you arrive in Spain.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <details>
                <summary>Can I travel internationally with only my TIE card?</summary>
                <div class="msv__faq-answer">
                    <p>No. The TIE is for identification within Spain only. For any international travel, you must carry your passport. Even with a long-term Spanish residence permit, your passport is your travel document. The TIE does not replace your passport.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <details>
                <summary>What happens if my TIE card expires?</summary>
                <div class="msv__faq-answer">
                    <p>Your TIE expires on the same date as your residence permit. If it expires, you must renew it immediately as part of renewing your residence permit. An expired TIE can affect your legal status, employment, and ability to access services. Always renew before expiration by starting the process 2–3 months in advance.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <details>
                <summary>Can I get a TIE before my residence permit is approved?</summary>
                <div class="msv__faq-answer">
                    <p>No. You can only apply for a TIE after your residence permit has been officially approved and you've entered Spain with it. You cannot apply speculatively. Wait until your residence permit is activated, then apply for the TIE within 30 days.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <details>
                <summary>What if I lose or damage my TIE card?</summary>
                <div class="msv__faq-answer">
                    <p>Report the loss or damage immediately to your local National Police headquarters. You'll need to apply for a replacement TIE card, which follows the same process and costs the same (€16). File a police report if it was lost or stolen, as this may be required. Temporary documentation may be issued while you wait for replacement.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

